//! The write-once rule for a stored period commitment.
//!
//! ## Why this is a module and not three lines in the contract
//!
//! Design B gives the verifier state, and that is a change to the security
//! story, so the rule governing it has to be *tested* -- and a rule written
//! inside `contract/` cannot be. `fe test` has no beacon-roots contract and
//! cannot call from the EIP-4788 system address, so nothing in a `recv` arm
//! is reachable from a test. The same reasoning that put the anchor source
//! behind a trait (`anchor::AnchorSource`) puts this here: the contract keeps
//! the storage, this module keeps the decision, and the decision is exercised
//! on both backends.
//!
//! ## What it is an exception to
//!
//! Invariant 1 says the anchor root is the only trusted input. A stored
//! summary root is a second thing a proof can start from, so the invariant
//! has to be restated rather than quietly stretched:
//!
//! > The anchor root is the only trusted **input**. A stored summary root is
//! > not an input: it is a value this code proved from an anchor root in an
//! > earlier transaction, admitted by one code path, write-once, and never
//! > read from calldata.
//!
//! Three properties carry that, and all three are load-bearing:
//!
//! 1. **Only a full proof admits a value.** `admit_summary_root` is called
//!    with `proven`, and the only `proven` in existence comes out of
//!    `verify_historical`: an EIP-4788 anchor root, the anchor header hashed
//!    against it, `fork.current_version` proven, the list length proven
//!    through the mix-in, the index bounded by it, and the branch landing
//!    exactly on `block_summary_root` at exactly its depth. There is no
//!    cheaper way in and no administrative one.
//! 2. **Write-once.** An admitted value can never become a different value.
//!    Every proof built on a stored root would be retroactively wrong if it
//!    could, so this is the property the whole design rests on.
//! 3. **Zero is not a value.** A `StorageMap` miss reads as zero, so zero has
//!    to mean "nothing stored" and nothing else.
//!
//! Re-proving the same period is allowed and changes nothing. It has to be:
//! two different blocks in one period are two historical proofs that both
//! reach the same `block_summary_root`, and the second must not revert.

/// The value to store for a period, given what is already there.
///
/// `stored` is the current entry -- zero if there is none -- and `proven` is a
/// commitment that has just been proven out of an anchor state. Returns the
/// value to write.
///
/// Reverts if `proven` disagrees with an entry already written. That can only
/// happen if SHA-256 has collided or a finalized period has been rewritten, so
/// it is not a case to recover from: it is a case to stop on.
pub fn admit_summary_root(stored: u256, proven: u256) -> u256 {
    // Property 3. A proven summary root is the hash tree root of 8,192 block
    // roots and cannot be zero, so this is unreachable -- which is why it is
    // an assertion and not a branch that returns something.
    assert!(proven != 0, "a zero summary root is not a commitment")

    if stored == 0 {
        // First time this period has been proven.
        proven
    } else {
        // Property 2. Idempotent for the same value, and closed for any other.
        assert!(
            stored == proven,
            "this period already has a different summary root",
        )
        stored
    }
}

/// Whether `admit_summary_root` would be writing a period for the first time.
///
/// The contract uses this to decide whether to emit its event, so that
/// re-proving a cached period is silent rather than looking like news.
pub fn is_first_admission(stored: u256) -> bool {
    stored == 0
}

// --- Tests ------------------------------------------------------------------

use ingot::fixture_historical_capella as historical

#[test]
fn an_empty_period_admits_a_proven_root() {
    assert!(
        admit_summary_root(stored: 0, proven: historical::BLOCK_SUMMARY_ROOT)
            == historical::BLOCK_SUMMARY_ROOT,
    )
    assert!(is_first_admission(stored: 0))
}

#[test]
fn re_proving_the_same_period_is_a_no_op() {
    // Two blocks in one period are two historical proofs reaching one summary
    // root. The second must be accepted and must not count as news.
    assert!(
        admit_summary_root(
                stored: historical::BLOCK_SUMMARY_ROOT,
                proven: historical::BLOCK_SUMMARY_ROOT,
            )
            == historical::BLOCK_SUMMARY_ROOT,
    )
    assert!(
        !is_first_admission(stored: historical::BLOCK_SUMMARY_ROOT),
    )
}

#[test(should_revert)]
fn a_written_period_cannot_be_rewritten() {
    // The property the whole cached path rests on. Every proof already built
    // on the stored root would become wrong if this returned.
    let _ = admit_summary_root(
        stored: historical::BLOCK_SUMMARY_ROOT,
        proven: historical::BLOCK_SUMMARY_ROOT ^ 1,
    )
}

#[test(should_revert)]
fn a_written_period_cannot_be_cleared() {
    // Zero is "nothing stored", so admitting it would turn a written period
    // back into an unwritten one.
    let _ = admit_summary_root(stored: historical::BLOCK_SUMMARY_ROOT, proven: 0)
}

#[test(should_revert)]
fn an_empty_period_does_not_admit_zero() {
    let _ = admit_summary_root(stored: 0, proven: 0)
}
